%I A401648 #6 Nov 05 2026 21:14:51
%S A401648 1,2,2,2,3,3,4,4,4,5,5,6,6,7,7,8,8,9,9,10,10,11,11,12,12,13,13,13,14,
%T A401648 14,15,15,15,16,16,17,17,17,18,18,19,19,19,20,20,21,21,21,22,22,23,23,
%U A401648 23,24,24,25,25,25,26,26,27,27,28,28,29,29,30
%N A401648 Earliest monotonic sequence satisfying a(1)=1 and a(a(n)+a(n-1)+a(n-2)+a(n-3)+a(n-4)) = n for n>=1 (with a(k)=0 for k<=0).
%C A401648 This is the almost Golomb sequence of order 5. The differences a(n+1)-a(n) are 0 or 1, every positive integer occurs, and each value m >= 3 first occurs at position a(m)+a(m-1)+a(m-2)+a(m-3)+a(m-4).
%C A401648 a(n) = A001462(n-1) for 3 <= n <= 11 = A001463(5), and a(12) = 6. Every value occurs at most 3 = A001462(4) times.
%C A401648 a(n)/n does not converge: a(5^k)/5^k -> 93/200 and a(2*5^k)/(2*5^k) -> 87/200.
%H A401648 Benoit Cloitre and Gandhar Joshi, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02404">Almost Golomb Sequences</a>, arXiv:2604.02404 [math.NT], 2026.
%F A401648 For n >= 3, with S(m) = a(m)+a(m-1)+a(m-2)+a(m-3)+a(m-4),
%F A401648 a(5*n)   = S(n) + 2 + e(n),
%F A401648 a(5*n+1) = S(n+1) + 1 - e(n),
%F A401648 a(5*n+2) = S(n+2) - 1 - h(n),
%F A401648 a(5*n+3) = S(n+3) - 4 + t(n),
%F A401648 a(5*n+4) = S(n+4) - 6 + u(n),
%F A401648 where e, h, t are 0-1 sequences with e(n)*h(n) = 0, u takes values in {0,1,2}, and all four are 5-automatic correction sequences with explicit 5-adic recurrences (see Cloitre and Joshi).
%e A401648 a(5)+a(4)+a(3)+a(2)+a(1) = 3+2+2+2+1 = 10 and a(10) = 5, as required for n = 5.
%o A401648 (PARI) A(N) = my(a=vector(N), m=3, p=5, L); a[1]=1; for(q=2, min(4,N), a[q]=2); while(p<=N, L=a[m+1]-if(m>=5, a[m-4]); for(q=p, min(p+L-1,N), a[q]=m); p+=L; m++); a
%Y A401648 Cf. A001462, A001463, A394217, A394218, A394219.
%K A401648 nonn,new
%O A401648 1,2
%A A401648 _Benoit Cloitre_, Oct 31 2026
